    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Что он сказал?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What did he say?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'what' with 'said'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он сказал нам уходить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He told us to leave.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'he' with 'to leave'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я не верю в это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not believe this.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'believe' to negate the action. Then Nastya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это конец?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is this the end?' Notice how 'this' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'end'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Я не знаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I do not know.' Chef Viktor links 'I' directly with 'not' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "maks",
      "t": "Шеф, что делать?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, what to do?' Max combines 'chef' with 'do' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Мы должны уйти.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We must leave.' Chef Viktor combines 'we' with 'to leave' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Nastya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мне очень жаль.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am very sorry.' Nastya links 'to me' directly with 'very much' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Собирайтесь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Pack your things.' Nastya replies with a single verb form meaning 'to intend' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Извините.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am sorry.' Nastya replies with a single verb form meaning 'sorry' — in Russian, the verb ending alone carries the subject and tense.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Это мой долг.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is my duty.' Nastya combines 'this' with 'debt' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
